I’m building an AI chat system where users can have long, continuous conversations with different LLM models.

The main challenge is maintaining high conversation quality while also keeping token usage and cost under control over time.

Since conversations can grow very large, sending the entire history on every request is not practical. At the same time, aggressive summarization can hurt the quality of the interaction.

This becomes even more challenging because different models have:

  * different context window sizes

  * different tokenization behavior

  * different input/output pricing

So a strategy that works well for one model may not be optimal for another.

I’m trying to understand:

What are the best proven patterns for managing short-term conversation context in production AI chat systems in a way that balances:

  * conversation quality

  * cost efficiency

  * scalability across many different LLM providers

Specifically:

  * How should raw messages vs summaries be balanced?

  * How should systems decide how much recent history to include?

  * Are there established architectural patterns for this problem?

I’m also very curious how systems like ChatGPT and Claude approach this internally when conversations become long.

I have many ideas but I’m looking for the best approach.

Has this problem been solved in a reusable or well-documented way by any team or open source project?

Memory-First Conversational Architecture as an Alternative to Long Context Windows

platypus February 11, 2026, 10:18pm 2

Hi @reza6 and welcome to the community.

There is a great cookbook on how to manage long running contexts here: Context Engineering for Personalization - State Management with Long-Term Memory Notes using OpenAI Agents SDK

I personally don’t use Agents SDK but to generalize what is in the cookbook - you essentially want to have some kind of a “compression” process on the side. The issue with summarization is that you lose a lot of information. So you really want a well designed prompt (and structure) for extracting key claims in a conversation. And I would always opt for a model like gpt-5.2 to perform something like that. You can store those in a markdown file (or JSON, or database). I find that this is really all you need.

A decision for when to initiate this claim extraction or compression can be anything really. But the easiest is some length of characters (e.g. 10k chars) in the messages (user-assistant turns). You can add some basic filtering where you look if one message was over some min length of characters (e.g. at least 5) but be careful with this as it may exclude numbers of super short answers (like “yes”).

